          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="5.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#fan-first" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Fix the Fan First</a>
          <a href="#how-we-chose" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Chose</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 5 Picks</a>
          <a href="#peltier-vs-compressor" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Peltier vs Compressor</a>
          <a href="#pint-ratings" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Pint Ratings and the 2019 DOE Change</a>
          <a href="#safety" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Electrical and Water Safety</a>
          <a href="#draining" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Draining and Placement</a>
          <a href="#mold" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Humidity Targets and Mold</a>
          <a href="#running-cost" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Settings and Running Cost</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most bathrooms do not need a dehumidifier. They need an exhaust fan that works, sized for the room and left running after the shower. A dehumidifier earns a place in a bathroom when there is no fan, the fan cannot be fixed easily, or the room stays damp for hours after everyone is dressed. When it does, the small plug-in units sold as bathroom dehumidifiers are usually too weak to help, and a compact 20 to 22 pint compressor model is the one that actually dries the room.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="fan-first" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Fix the Exhaust Fan First
          </h2>

          <p>
            A bathroom has a moisture problem for about twenty minutes a day, and then a lingering one if that moisture has nowhere to go. An exhaust fan solves the real problem by sending humid air outside before it soaks into drywall, grout and towels. A dehumidifier only treats the symptom, slowly, by pulling water out of air that is already in the room. If your bathroom has a fan, the first job is to check whether it works and whether it is big enough.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fan maker Broan cites the Home Ventilating Institute rule of thumb: about 1 CFM of fan capacity per square foot of floor for bathrooms up to 100 square feet, with a 50 CFM minimum. For larger bathrooms, HVI suggests adding 50 CFM per fixture, such as a toilet, a shower and a tub, and 100 CFM for a jetted tub. A typical 5-by-8-foot hall bath therefore needs at least a 50 CFM fan, and a 12-by-12 main bath with a separate shower and tub needs well over 100.
          </p>

          <p>
            Test your fan before you buy anything. Hold a single square of toilet paper against the grille with the fan on; if it does not hold the paper firmly, the fan is undersized, clogged with dust or venting into a crushed or disconnected duct. A fan that vents into the attic instead of outdoors is a common defect in older homes and moves the moisture problem into the roof. Cleaning the grille and impeller is free; replacing a weak fan with a quieter, properly sized one is a modest electrical job.
          </p>

          <p>
            Run the fan during the shower and for a while after; contractors commonly cite about 20 minutes as the target, and a humidity-sensing or timer switch makes that automatic. If the fan works, the door is cracked open after showers and the room still stays damp, then a dehumidifier makes sense. It also makes sense in a bathroom with no fan at all, or in a basement bathroom where the whole space runs damp, which is where our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">best basement dehumidifier</a> picks come in.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Ventilate first, dehumidify second.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A properly sized exhaust fan, roughly 1 CFM per square foot with a 50 CFM minimum per HVI guidance, removes shower moisture faster than any portable dehumidifier. Buy a dehumidifier only when the fan is missing, cannot be fixed or is not enough on its own.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How We Chose
          </h2>

          <p>
            The bathroom dehumidifier market is flooded with small plug-in units that promise to stop mold in your bathroom for the price of a dinner out. Most are thermoelectric, also called Peltier, units that remove a few ounces of water a day under ideal lab conditions. We included one honest example because some readers have a tiny powder room or a closet that suits them, but we did not let them dominate the list or pretend they can handle a full bathroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            For full bathrooms we looked at compact compressor dehumidifiers rated around 20 to 22 pints a day under the 2019 Department of Energy test, from brands with service networks and published spec sheets: Midea, Toshiba and Frigidaire. We favored small footprints, because bathroom floor space is scarce, and a continuous-drain connection, because a hose into the tub or shower drain means you never carry a sloshing bucket across a tiled floor.
          </p>

          <p>
            We gave weight to ENERGY STAR status and to how clearly the maker publishes capacity, bucket size and noise. Where a spec could not be confirmed, such as a decibel rating, we say so rather than borrow a number from a sibling model. We also checked that each model is still sold new, which ruled out several popular 22-pint units that their makers have discontinued.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, we included one product that is not a room dehumidifier at all, a cordless rechargeable desiccant unit, because the most common bathroom moisture complaint after mildew on the ceiling is a musty vanity cabinet or linen closet. It is the right tool for that job and the wrong one for the room. If you are still deciding what type and size to buy in general, our <a href="/how-to-choose-a-dehumidifier/">how to choose a dehumidifier</a> guide walks through it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Our 5 Bathroom Picks
          </h2>

          <p>
            These are real, currently sold models, ordered from the most capable to the most limited. We have not tested them in our own lab, so capacity, bucket and noise figures come from Midea, Toshiba, Frigidaire, Pro Breeze, Eva-Dry, ENERGY STAR or major retailers, and conflicting figures are flagged. Compressor capacities are 2019 DOE ratings; the small units are rated under much warmer, wetter test conditions, which the pint-ratings section explains.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <div id={product.id} class="scroll-mt-24 mt-12 first:mt-8">
              <div class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-sm hover:shadow-md transition-shadow">
                <div class="bg-gray-900 px-6 py-4 flex items-center justify-between">
                  <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
                    <span class="bg-[#FF4500] text-white w-8 h-8 rounded-full flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-black">
                      {index + 1}
                    </span>
                    {productImages[product.id] && (
                      <img
                        src={productImages[product.id]}
                        alt={product.name}
                        loading="lazy"
                        width="40"
                        height="40"
                        class="w-10 h-10 rounded-lg bg-white object-contain p-1 shrink-0"
                      />
