        <div id="peltier-vs-compressor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Peltier vs Compressor: The Capacity Gap
          </h2>

          <p>
            A thermoelectric dehumidifier uses a Peltier module, a small solid-state chip that gets cold on one side when current runs through it. A fan blows room air across the cold side, a little water condenses and drips into a small tank. There is no compressor, so the units are light, small and quiet. The trade-off is capacity. Pro Breeze rates its PB-02 at about 9 ounces a day and Eva-Dry rates its EDV-1100 at about 8 ounces, both at 86 degrees Fahrenheit and 80 percent relative humidity.
          </p>

          <p>
            A compressor dehumidifier works like a tiny air conditioner, with refrigerant, a compressor and a cold coil. The Midea Cube 20 is rated at 20 pints a day under the DOE test, and a pint is 16 ounces, so that is about 320 ounces a day under much cooler test conditions than the Peltier ratings. On paper that is more than 30 times the capacity of the small units. Even allowing for the different test conditions, the gap is enormous.
          </p>

          <p>
            That gap is why RTINGS, in its reviews of both the PB-02 and the EDV-1100, describes them as suited only to very small spaces and rates the Eva-Dry poorly for bathrooms. A hot shower puts enough vapor into a small bathroom to fog every mirror and bead the walls; a unit rated for about a cup of water a day under ideal conditions will not make a visible difference to that. What it can do is take the edge off slow, constant dampness in a very small space, such as a half bath with no shower.
          </p>

          <p>
            Peltier units also struggle in cool rooms. Their rated output assumes warm, very humid air, and output falls as the room cools. Compressor models have the opposite problem in cold spaces, which is frost on the coil below about 41 degrees Fahrenheit, but that is irrelevant in a heated bathroom. For any bathroom with a shower or tub, a compressor model is the only portable dehumidifier that will reliably dry the room.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Maker-Rated Capacity</p>
