        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Electrical and Water Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            A bathroom is the wettest room in the house, and a dehumidifier is a mains-powered appliance with a fan and, in compressor models, a few hundred watts of draw. The National Electrical Code has required ground-fault circuit interrupter protection for bathroom receptacles for decades, and that protection is your main defense. Before you plug anything in, press the test button on the outlet or the GFCI outlet upstream of it and make sure it trips and resets. If your bathroom outlet has no GFCI protection, have an electrician add it before using any appliance there.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep the dehumidifier out of the splash zone. Dehumidifier manuals commonly warn against using the unit where it can be splashed or stand in water, so never set one on the edge of the tub, inside a shower enclosure or on a floor that floods during baths. The best spot is usually against a wall away from the tub, on a dry floor, or just outside the bathroom door with the door open after showers. Do not run it from an extension cord; manuals for compressor units typically say to plug directly into a properly grounded outlet.
          </p>

          <p>
            Watch the cord and the hose. A cord across a bathroom floor is a trip hazard on wet tile, and a drain hose that pulls loose can leave the bucket overflowing or water on the floor. Route both along the wall, and check the hose connection after moving the unit. Unplug the unit before emptying the bucket or cleaning it, and dry your hands first; it sounds obvious, but bathrooms are where people handle appliances with wet hands.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cordless desiccant option avoids most of these issues, because the Eva-Dry E-333 runs with no power while it absorbs moisture and is only plugged in, elsewhere, to recharge. That is one reason we recommend it for a vanity cabinet or a linen closet. For the room itself, a compressor unit on a GFCI-protected outlet, away from the tub, is the safe setup.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="draining" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Draining and Placement
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bathrooms have one big advantage over basements: a drain is never far away. Most compact compressor dehumidifiers, including all three on our list, have a continuous-drain connection that takes a standard garden-hose-style tube. Run the hose to the tub or shower drain and the unit can run unattended without ever filling its bucket. Because these units have no pump, the drain works by gravity, so the outlet needs to sit above the drain with no upward loops in the hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice that means setting the unit on the floor next to the tub with the hose running over the rim and down to the drain, or on a low, sturdy stand beside a shower with a hose clip at the drain. Keep the unit itself dry and out of the splash zone. If gravity drainage is awkward, the bucket is the fallback; the Midea Cube 20 lists a 12-liter bucket, which is large for a unit this size, while the Frigidaire FHDD2233W1 holds 0.85 gallon. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-drain-a-dehumidifier-without-a-floor-drain/">draining a dehumidifier without a floor drain</a> covers other setups.
          </p>

          <p>
            Placement affects how well it works. A dehumidifier needs clear space around its intake and outlet, usually several inches to a foot depending on the manual, so do not wedge it between the toilet and the vanity. Close the door while it runs after a shower so it is drying the bathroom rather than the hallway, and leave a towel bar free so towels hung nearby dry faster. If the bathroom is very small, the unit can sit just outside the door with the door open, which also keeps it away from splashes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Noise is less of a concern in a bathroom than a bedroom, but it is not nothing. Frigidaire rates the FHDD2233W1 at 47.5 dBA on low and 49 on high, typical for the class. If the bathroom is an en suite and you want to run the dehumidifier overnight, look at our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/">best dehumidifier for bedrooms</a> guide, which focuses on maker-rated noise and sleep modes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="mold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Humidity Targets and Mold
          </h2>

          <p>
            The EPA recommends keeping indoor relative humidity below 60 percent, and ideally between 30 and 50 percent, to limit mold growth. In a bathroom, humidity will spike to near 100 percent during a shower no matter what you do; the goal is to bring it back below 60 percent quickly and keep it there between uses. Set a dehumidifier's humidistat to about 50 percent and let it cycle. A separate <a href="/best-hygrometer/">hygrometer</a> on a shelf tells you how long the room actually stays damp after a shower.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dehumidifier will not remove mold that is already growing. Black spots in grout, pink film around the drain and gray patches on the ceiling need cleaning with a bathroom mold cleaner, and stained or crumbling caulk usually needs replacing. Our <a href="/best-mold-remover/">best mold remover</a> picks cover the cleaning, and our <a href="/best-bathroom-caulk/">best bathroom caulk</a> guide covers the mold-resistant sealants worth using when you recaulk. Once surfaces are clean, the dehumidifier and fan keep them that way.
          </p>

          <p>
            The EPA also advises that mold covering more than about 10 square feet, mold inside walls, or mold that keeps coming back after cleaning may call for a professional. Recurring mold in the same spot often points to a leak behind the tile, a failed shower pan or condensation on an uninsulated exterior wall, and no dehumidifier fixes those. If you or someone in the home has asthma, allergies or a respiratory condition, talk to a doctor about mold exposure and about cleaning safely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some bathroom moisture is not from showers at all. A toilet tank that sweats in summer, a slow drip under the sink or a damp exterior wall can all keep humidity high. Check for these before assuming you need more drying power. If the whole house runs humid, a single bathroom unit is the wrong tool and a larger unit in the main living space, or the basement, is the better buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Settings and Running Cost
          </h2>

          <p>
            A bathroom dehumidifier does not need to run all day. The most efficient routine is to run it for an hour or two after showers, with the door closed, and let the humidistat shut it off once the room reaches your target. Units with a timer, such as the Toshiba with its 24-hour timer, make this easy. Smart units like the Midea Cube 20 can be scheduled or switched on from a phone, which is handy if everyone showers in the morning and leaves.
          </p>

          <p>
            ENERGY STAR lists the Midea Cube 20 at 249 kilowatt-hours a year under its standard assumptions, which assume far more running time than a bathroom typically needs. Your cost depends on your electricity rate and how many hours it actually runs. Our <a href="/how-much-electricity-does-a-dehumidifier-use/">dehumidifier electricity guide</a> shows how to estimate it from the wattage on the label. Peltier units draw little, around 22 watts for the EDV-1100 per Eva-Dry, but they remove so little water that their cost per pint is poor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Maintenance is simple but easy to forget in a bathroom. Rinse the bucket weekly so it does not grow slime, and wash the air filter every few weeks, more often if the bathroom is dusty or linty from towels. Hairspray and aerosol mist can coat the filter and coil, so do not run the unit next to where you style hair. Check the drain hose for kinks and algae now and then.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you find you need to run a dehumidifier for many hours a day just to keep a bathroom dry, that is a signal to revisit ventilation. A properly sized, properly ducted fan removes moisture for a fraction of the running cost and does it while you shower rather than afterward. The best bathroom setup uses both: the fan for the burst, the dehumidifier for the long tail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Dehumidifier Before Checking the Fan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A working exhaust fan, sized at about 1 CFM per square foot with a 50 CFM minimum per HVI guidance, removes shower moisture faster and cheaper than any portable. Test it with a square of toilet paper. If it fails, clean it, check the duct runs outdoors, or replace it before spending money on a dehumidifier.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting a Peltier Unit to Dry a Shower Room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small thermoelectric units are rated at 8 to 9 ounces a day under hot, humid lab conditions, about a cup of water. A hot shower puts far more vapor into a small room than that. They suit powder rooms and closets. For any bathroom with a shower or tub, buy a compressor model rated around 20 to 22 pints.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging Into an Unprotected Outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Bathroom receptacles should be GFCI-protected, and a dehumidifier is a mains appliance in the wettest room in the house. Test the GFCI before use, never run the unit from an extension cord, and keep it well away from the tub and shower where it could be splashed. If the outlet is not protected, call an electrician first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing Old and New Pint Ratings</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 2019 DOE test lowered ratings: an old 30-pint unit is now typically rated about 20 to 22 pints. Comparing a new 22-pint model with an old 30-pint review is comparing the same size machine. Check which standard each figure uses, and remember that Peltier ratings use far kinder conditions than either test.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating Mold With a Dehumidifier Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dehumidifier stops new growth by keeping humidity under control; it does not remove mold that is already there. Clean grout and ceilings with a proper mold cleaner, replace failed caulk, and fix leaks. The EPA suggests a professional for mold over about 10 square feet, inside walls, or that keeps returning after cleaning.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

