        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a dehumidifier in my bathroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not, if the bathroom has a working, properly sized exhaust fan that you run during and after showers. A dehumidifier makes sense when there is no fan, the fan cannot easily be fixed, or the room stays damp for hours afterward. A hygrometer will show you whether humidity drops back below 60 percent within an hour or so.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a dehumidifier in a bathroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, with precautions. Plug it directly into a GFCI-protected outlet, never an extension cord, and keep it away from the tub and shower where it could be splashed. Set it on a dry floor and route the cord and drain hose along the wall. Unplug it before emptying or cleaning, and always handle it with dry hands.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size dehumidifier do I need for a bathroom?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a full bathroom with a shower or tub, a compact compressor model rated around 20 to 22 pints a day under the 2019 DOE test is plenty. Small Peltier units rated in ounces per day are only suitable for powder rooms or closets. A 35 or 50 pint unit is oversized unless you are drying a whole damp basement suite.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do small bathroom dehumidifiers actually work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Small thermoelectric units do remove some water, but very little. Pro Breeze rates the PB-02 at about 9 ounces a day at 86 degrees and 80 percent humidity, and RTINGS describes such units as suited only to very small spaces. They help with mild, constant dampness in a tiny room, not with the steam from a daily shower.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I drain a dehumidifier into the bathtub?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, and it is one of the best things about using one in a bathroom. Most compact compressor units have a continuous-drain port; run the hose to the tub or shower drain. Without a pump, drainage is by gravity, so the outlet must sit above the drain with no upward loops. Keep the unit itself out of the splash zone.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What humidity should a bathroom be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The EPA recommends keeping indoor humidity below 60 percent, ideally 30 to 50 percent. A bathroom will spike near 100 percent during a shower, which is normal; the aim is to bring it back under 60 percent quickly. Setting a dehumidifier to about 50 percent and running the exhaust fan for 20 minutes or so after showering usually achieves that.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a dehumidifier get rid of bathroom mold?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. It helps prevent new mold by keeping humidity in check, but existing mold needs cleaning, and stained caulk usually needs replacing. Recurring mold in the same spot can mean a hidden leak or a cold exterior wall. The EPA suggests calling a professional for mold larger than about 10 square feet or inside walls.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

